# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# Git on Windows checks files out with CRLF by default. That is harmless for
# source we only ever read, and fatal for a file Linux has to *execute*: the
# shebang becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, the kernel looks for an interpreter literally
# named `sh\r`, and the container dies with
#
#     /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/01-app-role.sh: /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter
#
# which it did. Postgres then aborts initialisation, restarts, finds a data
# directory already present, skips the scripts entirely and comes up *healthy*
# without the application role ever having been created. The failure is one
# line deep in a log nobody reads and the symptom arrives later, as the API
# failing to authenticate against a database that looks fine.
#
# `eol=lf` on everything the container runs or reads means the bytes in the
# image are the bytes in the repository, whatever the developer's platform.

* text=auto

# Executed inside Linux containers.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker/** text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Read by tools that are whitespace-sensitive or that ship into images.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Compared byte for byte by the golden-value suite. Gzip is binary and must
# never be line-ending converted; the JSON beside it is diffed by people.
*.gz binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
